 "rulebook": "Official Baseball Rules (MLB, 2024 edition): 3.01 (the ball), Definitions of Terms: \"catch\" (secure possession in the hand before the ball touches the ground, held long enough to show control), \"in flight\"",
 "deviations": [
  "Definitions (catch): no glove; the hand catches by a grip that closes on a ball whose surface is within 3.5 cm of the palm and that moves no faster than 6 m/s relative to it (the platform's grip). \"Held long enough\" is 1.0 s without letting go and without falling. A ball that touches the body (any part other than the hands and forearms) before it is gripped is not a catch here (the rule would allow it).",
  "The fly ball is not batted: a launcher 40.0 m away sends it at 21.35 m/s, 45.0 degrees above the horizon, 1.0 s after the start, without spin; where it comes down (within 3.0 m of where you stand) is this instance's draw.",
  "Flight: quasi-steady aerodynamics (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model: drag and Magnus lift), still air."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Catch the fly ball: grip the baseball with a hand before it touches the ground or your body, and keep hold of it.",
  "rules": "A baseball (0.073 m across, 0.145 kg, it flies in air with drag) rests on a launcher 40.0 m in front of you, 1.0 m above the ground, and is launched at t = 1.0 s at 21.35 m/s, 45.0 degrees above the horizon, 1.18 degrees to the left of the line from the launcher to you, so that untouched it would come down within 3.0 m of where you stand at the start. A catch is a hand's grip closing on the ball in flight after it has passed the top of its arc: the grip holds a ball whose surface is within 3.5 cm of the palm and that moves no faster than 6 m/s relative to it. Before the grip the ball may touch only your hands and forearms. You succeed when a hand has gripped the ball and kept it for 1.0 s without a break. You fail if the ball touches the ground, or any part of you other than a hand or a forearm, before it is gripped; if you let go of it after the catch; if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.5 m above the ground, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground) before the end; or after 9 s.",
  "venue": "You stand on a level field facing the launcher (+x), 40.0 m away. The ball rises to about 11 m and comes down steeply, about three seconds after the launch, somewhere within 3.0 m of you. Nothing can be gripped before the launch. Still air.",
  "difficulty": "The ball is in the air for three seconds and comes down at about 17 m/s more than a metre away: the body has to walk there at 0.6 m/s and put a palm on the ball's line to within a few centimetres, facing it, with the grip closing in the few milliseconds in which the ball is against the palm."
 },
